$ do phase("night", 3)
The Prince has not said when the evening ends, and nobody in the house will be so bad mannered as to suggest it. There are four things in the rooms that want doing, by your own reckoning, and time for three of them before the hour at which a polite guest begins to look at the windows.
-> night

# drive
## The faubourg
The week has gone in the shop and the street. On Thursday, at the hour when the city goes to the theatre, a black coach with no arms on the door comes up the rue Saint-Jacques at a walk and stops before the sign of the sun. Octave is in it, with his hat on his knee. He does not say a word until the shutters of the Latin quarter are behind you, and then he says, to the window, that the Prince is in a good humor and that you should not be deceived by it.
The great houses of the faubourg Saint-Germain stand behind high walls, in long streets that are all gate and no window, and a coach with its blinds down goes through them as a thought goes through a sleeping head. Over the river the Opera is lit, and you can see, as you cross, the long double line of carriages on the quay with their lamps, and the footmen asleep on the boxes, and the breath of two hundred horses going up in a single gray cloud. It is the hour, Octave says, at which Paris is looking the other way. Everything that the court wishes to do without being seen it does between the second act and the ballet. It has done so for a hundred and fifty years, and nobody has ever once remarked that the box of the Marquise de Sérignan is empty for the whole of the evening.
Frost has put a skin on the cobbles, and the horses go carefully. Octave says that you are expected. He says that the Prince has asked for you by name, in the old way, which is a courtesy that has not been shown to anyone since the old Duke. He says, a moment later, and without turning his head, that the Prince is not a man who wastes a courtesy.
<~

# hunger
## Before the gate
The hunger comes up in the dark of the coach, in the long stretch of the quai des Théatins, and you would very much sooner it did not. It is a bad thing to arrive at the house of a Prince with an empty vein. It is a worse thing to do it in the street.
Octave has seen your face. He taps on the roof, and the coach stops in the shadow of a wall, and he says, with the gentleness of a man who has often done it for others, that there is a house close by where a woman is paid by the month to be asleep.
* Take what you need from the woman who is paid to sleep
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is very quiet, and very clean, and the house smells of lavender and old linen. She does not wake, and she will have a headache in the morning, and a small purse under the pillow. It is the way that the court does it, and it is a great deal better than the way that you have known. You do not like how easily you accept it.
* Ask the coachman to stop at a stable, and take it from a horse
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ secrecy += 1
  The coachman does not turn a hair. A horse is a horse, and he has been driving gentlemen in black for thirty years. The horse is a big gray with a patient eye. Octave holds the lantern, and averts his face, and says afterward that it is a very fine old-fashioned habit and that nobody in the house will ever forgive you for it.
* Go in as you are, and say nothing
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  It is a long hour. You sit with your hands on your knees and your teeth together, and Octave looks at them and says nothing at all, for which you are grateful. In the great gilded hall of the Prince there will be a number of people who can tell to a minute how long it is since you last fed. You are going to be looked at, a good deal, by people who are hungry in a much more comfortable way.
<~

# hotel
## The Hotel de Saint-Aubin
There is a court before the house, and a stair, and a porter in the livery of a bank, who bows without lifting his eyes. The Hôtel de Saint-Aubin is a great white house of the last reign, with a roof like a folded fan, and a staircase that was meant to be looked at from below. It is lit from top to bottom. It is, you realize, lit as a ship is lit that has been told that it will be fired on: every window, every landing, a hundred and fifty candles on the stair alone, and not a single person in the street outside who has the faintest idea that anything is going on.
Inside, it is warm. It is very warm, and smells of orange water and wax and hot sugar, and under it, faintly, a good deal older than any of it, of what you would expect. The court has gone to a great deal of trouble to make its dead into a spectacle. There are cakes. There are tiers of them on the sideboards, in pale pink and gold and green, in the shape of temples and ships and swans, and a great many of the guests are standing in front of them with a small silver fork, not eating. It is a thing that Octave explains in the stairwell, very low. It is the fashion. It is a way of showing that one has everything and does not need it.
There are perhaps sixty of them in the long salon. They have the faces of people who were beautiful at nineteen and have not been allowed to forget it, and they turn, all together, as you come in, with the soft movement of a flock of birds. It is the very reverse of the cellar at Careggi. There is not a plain chair in the room. There is, at the far end, under a great chandelier, a small gray man in a coat of black velvet who has not got up.
He is seated on a straight wooden chair that would not be out of place in a convent. He has a book on his knee. He has been sitting so, you understand, since before you came in, and will continue until you are gone, and everybody in the room knows exactly where he is without once looking. He lifts his pale eyes, and they find you at once.
"Monsieur Colline," says Gualtiero, Prince of the court of France, who was once Prince of Florence. "It is a very long way from the Via Larga. I am glad to see that the road did not wear you."
$ do chart("paris.court")
$ do meet("serignan")
$ do meet("brezac")
~> prince_welcome
<~

# prince_welcome
?if b2.court_end == "seat"
  "I had thought," says the Prince, "that you would be gone for a century and that I should have to tell the council that your chair was empty. It is a pleasure to be wrong." There is, you notice, a chair at his left hand, and it is not a plain one. It is kept for you. It has been kept, you gather, for three hundred and ten years, and dusted.
?elif b2.court_end == "debt"
  "It is a very rare thing," says the Prince, "to be owed a debt by a man who is certain to remember it." He smiles, thinly. "I should like to say at once that I have not forgotten, and that I am not going to ask you to forgive it. It is not the business of this evening."
?else
  "It is a quality that I have always admired in you," says the Prince, "that you want nothing. I have been a prince for four hundred years, and I have never been able to learn it."
?end
He lets that hang in the candlelight. Then he lowers his eyes to the book and says, in an absent voice, that you are to enjoy the house, that you are to be introduced to such of his friends as you like, and that he will send for you when the music has begun, which is the only hour at which a man in his position can be sure of not being overheard.
It is, you think, very civil. It is the civility of a man who has had a good many guests and has not once been wrong about any of them.
<~

# salon
## The long salon
The Marquise de Sérignan is a woman of no age whatever, in black silk and a good deal of diamonds, with white hair dressed three feet high in the style of a queen who has been dead for a decade. She has been at the court since it crossed the Alps. She was made in the reign of Henri Deux, in a room that she will describe to you, if you let her, and she has the gift of making a very long story seem as if it had been told for you alone. She takes your arm and walks you down the room with the air of a woman showing off a tame lion to a hostile audience.
"You will be very much stared at," she says. "It is in your favor. We have not had anything to stare at since the English ambassador's parrot died."
She tells you the court. It does not take her long. There is a king who is good, and a queen who is Austrian, and a minister from Geneva who is going to make everything right, and, beneath the three of them, a country that is cold and hungry and has begun to read. She says that the court has lived in France for two hundred and fifty years and has never, in all that time, been so afraid.
"They do not tell you," she says, "that it is a very uncomfortable thing to be hunted by arithmetic."
She has a manner that you have met before, in the best of the Merchant's people, of asking a question as if she knew the answer and was only checking that you did.
* Be charming, and let her think that she has found a friend
  $ b3.salon = "charmed"
  $ standing += 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  It is not difficult. You have had three centuries to learn how it is done and she has had two hundred and fifty years to forget how it is received. She laughs, behind her fan, in a high clear voice that does not belong to a woman of her years, and half the room looks round. By the end of the second tour you have been introduced to eleven people and have been invited to nine suppers. She says, in your ear, that you are a great deal too good at it, and she says it with a kind of delight.
* Be civil and cold, and give nothing away
  $ b3.salon = "cold"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  She takes it very well. She has been refused by better. She says that it is a great pity, because she had been going to be extremely indiscreet, and she pats your hand and lets you go. A number of people, behind her, have noticed that you did not laugh. You are aware, as you cross the room, of the kind of silence that follows a man who has been judged and found to be of no use at a card table.
* Say little, and listen, which is what the room is for
  $ b3.salon = "listened"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 2
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is the old method, and it has never failed. You ask her about the king. You ask her about the Prince's health. You ask her, as a thing of no importance, which of the families are in debt to the house. She tells you. She tells you with such evident joy that you perceive that nobody else has asked her anything for years. By midnight you have a map of the room in your head, with the lines of fear drawn in, and it is the single most useful thing that you will carry out of the house.
A very young man has been standing at the end of the sideboard for some time, in a coat of lilac silk, with his hands behind him, pretending to be interested in a swan made of sugar. He has a handsome narrow face, and a very high color for a dead man, and the abstracted look of someone who has been told to wait. The Marquise sees him at the same moment, and her face closes like a book. "That," she says, "is the Chevalier de Lasalle. He is the Prince's godchild. He is very amusing. I should not be seen to enjoy it."
She leaves you.
He comes along the sideboard in his own time. He does not bow. He looks at the swan and at you, and says, to the swan, in a tone of the most perfect politeness, that he hopes the little card was of some use.
You look at him. "The pencil," says Séverin de Lasalle. "On the back. I thought that you would like a second opinion before you were asked for a first."
$ do meet("severin")
* Thank him, and ask what he knows
  $ b3.severin = "open"
  $ bond.severin += 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  He looks pleased, and then, a little too late, afraid, as if he had not meant to be seen being pleased. "Not here," he says. "I will tell you what I can. I would sooner tell you the truth than a story, but I should like you to be the first to say which you prefer." He drifts off toward the cakes. He leaves behind him a faint smell of violets and powder, and a distinct feeling of having been, in some manner, auditioned.
* Thank him, and say nothing more
  $ b3.severin = "careful"
  $ secrecy += 1
  "Very wise," says Séverin, gravely, and means it, and is a little hurt by it. He bows, at last, a good deal lower than the occasion needs. "I will be in the house all evening. Everybody is. It is the great thing about us." He goes off, and you realize that you have just been handed, with great skill, to the one person in the room who is not looking at you.
* Ask whose card it was, and whether he writes it to everyone
  $ b3.severin = "cold"
  $ bond.severin -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  The color goes out of his face by a degree. "To a few," he says. "To the ones that I think are worth the postage." He has the look of someone who has put his hand into a drawer and found it empty. He bows, and goes, and the swan loses a wing to his sleeve on the way, and you think that you have made a small enemy of a person who did not deserve it, and a great deal more likely a friend.
<~

# gallery
## The long gallery
The Prince's champion is called Brézac, and has been the sword of the court since before the Fronde. He is a big, fair, quiet man of about forty, in a plain coat of gray, with a scar from the corner of his mouth to his ear that nobody has ever had the bad manners to ask about. The long gallery is lit by two hundred candles and floored with oak. The walls are hung with swords. It is a place that was built for exactly one purpose, and the court is already gathering in the doorway to see it done.
"The Prince," says Brézac, "does not ask for proof. It is a thing that he never does. He asks me to say that if you should happen to feel like a little exercise, it would be a kindness to the young ones." He does not say it unkindly. He has a pleasant voice, with a trace of the Midi. He is a soldier asking another soldier if he would care to look at the ground.
There is no way to make it more than it is. He is an elder, a man of two hundred years who has done nothing else, and the whole court knows what it is being offered: the first look, in three hundred years, at a man of the Merchant's line who does not belong to anyone.
* Accept, and give him the whole of it
  ~> trial
* Accept, to first blood, and say so before the room
  $ standing += 1
  ~> trial_light
* Decline, courteously, as a guest who has not come to be tried
  $ b3.trial = "declined"
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy += 2
  Brézac bows. There is a murmur, in the doorway, like the settling of silk. "Very proper," he says, "and very wise," and sets his sword back on the wall as carefully as a man who has no wish to be seen to be disappointed. You can feel the room revise you downward by a very small amount and upward by a very large one. It is the kind of calculation that a court does without ever knowing it has done it. You have been placed in the category of those who do not need to prove anything, which is the most dangerous one.
<~

