# trial_end
?if fight_result() == "won"
  $ b3.trial = "won"
  $ standing += 3
  $ secrecy -= 2
  It ends with his sword flat against the floor, and yours at the point of his collar, and a silence that you could cut and serve. Brézac looks up at you with an expression of the purest professional interest. "A very great pleasure," he says. Somebody in the doorway begins to clap, very quietly, and stops, as if he had been caught at it. You have been seen, in front of sixty of the court, to have done a thing that has not been done in this house since the Fronde.
?elif fight_result() == "talked"
  $ b3.trial = "won"
  $ standing += 2
  It comes to you in the middle of a lunge, and you say it, and he stops. It is a very short sentence, about the weather in Florence in the year that he was made. His blade comes down by an inch, and he looks, for a moment, thirty years younger. "Monsieur," he says, "that was not fair." He is laughing. The court does not know quite what to do with it.
?elif fight_result() == "fled"
  $ b3.trial = "lost"
  $ standing -= 2
  You leave the floor and leave the gallery, and nobody tries to stop you, which is much worse than if they had. Brézac does not follow. He puts up his sword and says, to nobody, that it was well done to know when. It is a very polite remark. It is the kind of remark that a court spends a month repeating.
?else
  $ b3.trial = "lost"
  $ do heal()
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  It is a short fight and you lose it. He does it with great grace, and a certain regret, and he gives you a hand to rise. "Thirty years ago," he says, quietly, "I should not have been so careful." The court makes the small appreciative noise that it makes when it has seen a good thing well done. You find that you do not mind, as much as you ought. You have found out the limit of a thing, and in this house that is worth a good deal.
?end
<~

# garden
## The orangery
Octave takes you out through a window into a glass house at the back, where the court keeps its oranges. It is the one room in the house that is not warm. It is very dark, and smells of earth and leaf and damp, and he walks down the central path between the tubs with his hat in his hands, the way a man walks in a church where he is no longer sure of his place.
"I am going to tell you something," he says, "and I would take it as a kindness if you did not say whom you had it from." He has aged. You know that it is not possible. It is the look of a man who has been looked at too long.
He tells it in the plain order of a clerk. There is a Prince who has not slept for six weeks, and who sits in his chair at night with a book that he does not read. There is a court that has decided, without ever saying so, that if the city rises it will be the city's fault and that somebody ought to be sent to see to it. There is a Senate in Rome, which has not forgotten, and which has sent a Lictor to Paris this autumn on a matter of the Remembrancer's roll. He is an old one. He has taken a room in the rue des Prouvaires over a cutler's. He has been seen in the Faubourg, at night, in a plain brown coat, asking in a courteous voice after a certain kind of Roman face.
"His name," says Octave, "is Galerius. I think that you know it."
You do. It is a very long way from the Via Sacra. There is a silence between the tubs, in which a drop of water falls from a leaf.
"I should like," says Octave, "to be of use to you. It is not a thing that I can say in the house."
* Promise him that if it comes to it, he will not be left to the Prince's anger
  $ b3.octave = "promised"
  $ bond.ottaviano += 3
  $ humanity += 1
  He does not answer. He holds his hat against his chest, and for a moment his shoulders move. "I have been a servant for six hundred years," he says, at last. "No one has ever said that before. I shall try not to disappoint you." He puts his hat on. There is a dry sound in it, like a small bird landing on a roof, and you realize that it is the first time in a very long while that he has made a promise of his own.
* Take what he has told you, and give nothing back
  $ b3.octave = "guarded"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ bond.ottaviano -= 1
  "Of course," says Octave, and nods, very slightly, as a man nods at a tradesman's bill. He is not offended. He has done exactly this to others, in a long career, and it is not unfair that it should be done to him. He goes back to the house with his hat straight and a look of having been, on the whole, thoroughly dealt with.
* Ask him to keep an ear at the Prince's door, and tell you what is said there
  $ b3.octave = "spy"
  $ bond.ottaviano -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  He looks at you for a long time. Then he says that he has been listening at the Prince's door for three hundred and fifty years, and that he had not, until now, known that it might be of any value to anybody. It is said without bitterness. It is said as a man says a thing that he has just understood about his own life. "I will do it," he says, "but I wish that you had not asked." You wish it too.
$ do discover("the_lictors_of_paris")
