# roll
## The Remembrancer's desk
It is in a small room off the library, behind a tapestry, with a lamp and a clerk who is asleep. It is the roll of the court. It is a great calf bound book, as long as a man's arm, ruled in red, in which every debt, every favor, every secret and every gift of the vampires of Paris has been entered for two hundred and fifty years in the same small black hand.
The clerk, who is old, and has the gray patient face of a creature that lives at a desk, wakes in the lamplight and gives a great start. He says that it is not allowed. He says it three times. Then he sees what he is looking at, and does not say it a fourth.
It is a good book. It is the best that you have seen since the Banca. In the long left column is every name in the room, and in the long right column what each name owes, and to whom, and for what; and at the bottom of a good many of the pages, in a neater hand, in green, a line that says *Amsterdam*. It is a very considerable sum. It is, you realize, a very considerable fraction of the whole of the court's capital, and it is all owed to a single house in a single city, in the name of a woman who has not set foot in France in a hundred years.
You find the Prince's page last. He is not in debt to Amsterdam. He has never been. He is, instead, the creditor of the whole of the court, which is exactly what you would expect. It is only on the last line, half under the binding, that you find the single thing that does not fit, a small regular quarterly sum paid by the Prince, in gold, to an account at Avignon, to a name that is not a name but a title, written out in full in a very old form of French. *The Lady who sleeps.*
* Copy the page, and put it back
  $ b3.roll = "read"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ do discover("the_roll")
  You do it as you have done it in a dozen archives, with a small blunt pencil on the back of a playbill. The clerk watches with the patient horror of an old man watching his own execution. When you give him the book he holds it against his chest like an infant. "I did not see you," he says. "I should like it to be understood." It is understood. It is the sort of thing that is understood very quickly in this house.
* Close the book, and give it back to the clerk unread
  $ b3.roll = "left"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ standing += 1
  The clerk's relief is so great that it is almost indecent. He takes the book, and says that he will remember the courtesy. It is a thing that you suspect will be remembered, in a house where the whole of life consists of what is remembered. You walk out of the little room with the feeling of having refused something, and of not being at all certain what.
<~

