# prince
## The request
It is a small room at the very top of the house. It has a fire, and two chairs, and a window that looks east over the roofs of the city, toward the faubourg Saint-Antoine, where there are no lights at all. There is a table with a decanter that nobody has touched. The Prince has put down his book.
"I shall be brief," he says, "because it is a long evening and we are both very old." He folds his hands. "It is a plain thing and I shall say it plainly. I am afraid."
It is, you think, a remarkable thing to say. He says it as a man reports the weather.
"The king is a good man who has never in his life been asked to decide anything. The ministers are afraid of the crowds, and the crowds are afraid of the ministers, and the army, I am told by a very honest officer, would shoot at a general sooner than at a baker. There is going to be a great meeting in May. There will be a fair, and a riot, and a revolution, probably in that order, and my court has no idea at all what to do about it." He pauses. "That is the first fear. It is the one I can say aloud."
He looks, then, out of the window. "The second is nearer. There are young ones in the faubourg. They have read the pamphlets. They have been taught, by a good many printers, that a man may be anything that he is clever enough to be, and they have drawn the obvious conclusion. They do not trust my Peace. They think it is a cage." He smiles, without humor. "They are right."
There is a silence. The fire shifts.
"I need a hand outside the court," says Gualtiero. "I do not mean a sword. I have Brézac for that, who is better than anything that you or I could make. I mean someone old, and strong, and of no party, whom the young ones might trust and the old ones might fear. I mean someone whom I could send. I should like it to be you." He lifts his eyes. "I have never in my life said the plain word. It is a weapon. I am asking you to be mine."
?if b3.roll == "read"
  You think of a small black line, half under a binding, and a title written out in full, and you do not look at it. It is a very odd sensation to hold a man's secret in front of him like a cup.
?end
?if b3.trial == "won"
  He does not mention the gallery. He does not need to. The whole of the house has been talking of nothing else for an hour.
?elif b3.trial == "lost"
  He does not mention the gallery. It is, you realize, a courtesy, and a measure, and a fair one.
?end
:the_ask
* "I will be your hand. Tell me what you want done."
  $ b3.weapon = "accepted"
  $ standing += 3
  $ defiance -= 2
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  He closes his eyes, for a moment, in what you would almost call relief. "It is not much," he says. "It is only a list." He takes a folded paper from his sleeve and lays it on the table between you, face down, without a word. It is a very old gesture, and it has been made in this house a great many times. You do not turn it over, yet. You can feel the weight of it, as a man feels a stone in his pocket that he has agreed to carry across a country.
  He is, you think, much older than he looked when you came in.
* "I will be your hand, at a price. Let us speak of the price."
  $ b3.weapon = "bargained"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ standing += 1
  ~> price
* "No. I will not be anyone's weapon."
  $ b3.weapon = "refused"
