# dawn
## The coach
$ do discover("the_estates_general")
The coach takes you home along the river, in the black before the dawn, with the blinds down and Octave silent in the corner. The frost has hardened. The Seine is beginning to freeze, in long gray plates that grind against the piers of the Pont Neuf like teeth, and the bakers' lamps are lit in the rue Dauphine, and a long line of women has formed at every door in the cold. Nobody in it looks at the coach. They have no reason to.
?if b3.weapon == "accepted"
  There is a folded paper in your coat. It has twelve names on it, in a clerk's hand. It is the list of the young ones in the faubourg who are to be spoken to. You do not read it in the coach. You know that you will read it at the first quiet moment, and that you will be sorry.
?elif b3.weapon == "bargained"
  You have a bargain, and a name for it, and a promise that is in a house's keeping. You are not at all sure whether you have bought a friend or sold a year, and you suspect that the difference will become clear in the spring.
?else
  The door was left open, as he had said. It is a very great deal lighter to go out of a house than to go into it. It is only on the Pont Neuf, with the river grinding under the wheels, that you begin to consider what kind of silence it was that had been left behind you.
?end
?if b3.severin == "open"
  Somewhere between the Pont Neuf and the rue Saint-Jacques, a paper is pushed into the coach through the open slat of the blind by a hand in a lilac glove, and falls on the seat. It is a card, with an address in the faubourg Saint-Antoine, and under it a single line: *If you wish to know what they are afraid of, come on Sunday. Bring nobody. Bring a lantern.* Octave says nothing. He looks at the card for a long moment and then, very carefully, out of the window.
?end
Étienne is waiting up. He has a candle, and a face the color of tallow, and he has plainly not been to bed. He says that Captain Aubry has been out twice to see if the street was clear. He says that Baptiste has sat the whole night in the shop with a stick, and that Adélaïde has done the accounts. He says that nobody has been taken. It is a very good thing to be able to say. You tell him that nobody will be.
You say it, and as you say it, you hear what it will mean.
